# Board profiles for the build scripts

One file per composite board: the provider each platform interface is bound
to, and the driver whose symbols stand behind it. The build scripts source a
profile through mm_load_board and check an image against it, so a board's
facts are written once instead of in every script that builds for it.

The lane -- target, compiler, SDK -- is not here. scripts/lib/pico.sh finds it
by walking the board's derives-from chain in the manifests to one of the six
boards the Pico SDK bridge recognises. A profile carries only what no manifest
states: which driver's symbols prove an interface reached real hardware code.

Each profile sets board_interfaces, the interfaces the board binds, and for
each one provider_<interface> and, where a driver stands behind it,
driver_<interface>. The values are checked by the scripts that use them, not
generated; the model already knows every board's bindings, and generating
these from it is a later step.
